  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nThe next upper level system and associated trough axis will race \nacross Canada and Northern Plains through the short term. In \nresponse to said upper level system, low level flow will increase \nout of the south, advecting moisture across the region. \nEnsemble guidance shows precipitable water values 150-200 \npercent of normal overlaying the region through the day with DP \ntemps in the mid to upper 50s to around 60 degrees across \nwestern MN. As a result, rain chances increase this morning with\nscattered to widespread showers. High res model guidance \ndepicts this swath of showers moving SW to NE through the \nmorning, bringing it into the metro by 10am(SW metro) to \n12pm(NE metro) timeframe. Shower activity will be more \nwidespread across western MN, with lesser probabilities and \ncoverage across eastern MN and western WI. Expected rainfall \namounts will range from 0.30-0.70 inches across western MN and \nless than 0.30 inches across eastern MN and western WI. Forecast\nsoundings show a sliver of elevated CAPE across western MN, \ncan't rule out a rumble of thunder embedded within the shower \nactivity. High temperatures on Saturday will trend cooler with \nhighs in the low to middle 60s, middle to upper 60s across \nwestern/central WI.\n\nOn Sunday, the aforementioned upper level system and associated \nmoisture will quickly translate east. Upper level ridge axis \nwill settle in it's wake across the region with dry conditions \nexpected throughout the day, a remnant isolated shower or two \npossible during the morning hours. High temperatures rebound \nback up into the upper 60s. Surface high pressure will settle in\nover the Great Lakes region. Being on the western periphery, \nsoutherly flow will remain in place, with DP temps remaining \naround 60 degrees.",
